  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several surface-design strategies used to control how liquid droplets form, grow, and move on textured materials. Across all panels, blue droplets sit on microstructured surfaces, and the key message is that wetting behavior can be tuned by chemistry, roughness, or geometry.\n\nSome examples focus on where droplets nucleate and how they spread. Hydrophilic regions appear to promote easier droplet formation than hydrophobic ones, while “mixed mode” surfaces combine off-site and on-site nucleation so droplets can grow in preferred locations. A wicking substrate seems to draw liquid through the texture, feeding droplets and creating directed flow.\n\nOther panels emphasize droplet mobility. In the SLIPS concept, a rough surface infused with lubricant creates a smooth lubricating layer that reduces pinning, so droplets can slide more readily than on a bare rough surface. A wettability gradient, transitioning from hydrophilic to hydrophobic, drives droplet motion along the surface. A geometric surface-energy gradient similarly uses changing feature geometry to create directional movement, indicated by arrows.\n\nOverall, the figure shows that engineered micro/nanotextures can regulate condensation and transport by controlling nucleation sites, growth, wetting, and self-propelled droplet motion.",
